      "dom_id": "question-6728",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196342/assets/998d9b7d5d9e342f58add32f80cddfcb6f01e255497f87376c0e39e01abb67ba.png)\n\nIs the above rule a function?",
      "worked_solution": "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196342/assets/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196342/assets/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value.",
