    {
      "dom_id": "question-31655",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n| **$x$** | $-6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | ? | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $30$ | $26$ | $1$ | ? | $3$ | $12$ | $28$ |",
      "worked_solution": "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\n- If we substitute the $xy$-pair $(3,2)$ into our table, we get a function.\n\n| **$x$** | $-6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $30$ | $26$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ | $28$ |\n\nNotice that all values in the first row are *distinct* (i.e., different). Every input value is assigned to *precisely one* output value.\n\n- None of the other $xy$-pairs gives rules that are functions. So, for example, substituting the $xy$-pair $(4,12)$ gives a rule that is *not* a function. \n\n| **$x$** | $-6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $30$ | $26$ | $1$ | $12$ | $3$ | $12$ | $28$ |\n\nNotice that the first row contains the value $x=4$ twice. The input value $4$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($3$ and $12$).",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
